        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.069,
            0.816,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Measuring Space: Perimeter and Area"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "119"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.11,
            0.472,
            0.132
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.1 Perimeter of a Shape"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.14,
            0.886,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "Given any shape, its **perimeter** is the total length around its border. Imagine a tiny insect going for a walk around its border, never turning around, till it returns to its starting point. The perimeter of the shape is the total distance it travels."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.24,
            0.457,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "So, a square with side a units has perimeter 4a units. An equilateral triangle with side a units has perimeter 3a units."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.508,
            0.221,
            0.632,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.527,
                0.33,
                0.61,
                0.349
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.2 A"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.697,
            0.216,
            0.851,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.736,
                0.33,
                0.819,
                0.349
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.2 B"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.356,
            0.885,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": "The perimeter of a rectangle with length a units and width b units is $2 ( a + b )$ units. Note that the formula for the perimeter of a square is a ‘special case’ of the formula for the perimeter of a rectangle with a = b."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.426,
            0.321,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.215,
                0.532,
                0.283,
                0.55
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.3"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.471,
            0.858,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": "Here we see a circle with radius r units. What is its perimeter? How do we find out?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.572,
            0.399,
            0.596
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.609,
            0.855,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": "What is the connection between this question and the one about the 400 m athletics track?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.664,
            0.884,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": "To answer the question about the perimeter of the circle, we must go step by step."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.713,
            0.478,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "What happens to the perimeter of a square if we double its side? It doubles too. The ratio of perimeter to the side is 4:1; this is so for all squares. As the side of the square gets larger (or smaller), the ratio of perimeter to side stays fixed at 4:1."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.485,
            0.704,
            0.875,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.514,
                0.892,
                0.845,
                0.91
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.4: Ratio of perimeter to side is 4:1"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_17.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
